## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design runtime-extendable components using a simple plugin registry, mirroring real-world maintenance and extension needs.

 ## Key Concepts

- Plugin interface and duck typing
- Runtime registry and manifest loading
- Execution order and error handling
- Testing plugin isolation

 ## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal plugin interface via duck typing
class Plugin {
  process(line) { return line; }
}

class RedactPlugin { process(line) { return line.replace(/password=\S+/g, 'password=REDACTED'); } }
class TimestampPlugin { process(line) { return `[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${line}`; } }

class Registry {
  constructor() { this.factories = new Map(); }
  register(name, factory) { this.factories.set(name, factory); }
  create(name) { const f = this.factories.get(name); return f ? f() : null; }
}

// Boot: register plugins
const registry = new Registry();
registry.register('redact', () => new RedactPlugin());
registry.register('timestamp', () => new TimestampPlugin());

// Manifest-driven sequence
const manifest = ['redact', 'timestamp'];
let line = 'user=alice password=hunter';
for (const name of manifest) {
  const plugin = registry.create(name);
  if (plugin) line = plugin.process(line);
}
console.log(line);
```

